course: religion-ethics
lesson: 30
title: Judaism
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Judaism is the faith of the Jewish people, who believe in one God who made a covenant with Abraham and gave the Torah to Moses.
explain: Christianity grew out of Judaism: Jesus, Mary and the first disciples were Jewish, and Christians read the Hebrew Bible as their Old Testament.
explain: Jewish life has its own rhythm: Shabbat each week from Friday sunset to Saturday sunset, Passover in spring with the Seder meal, Hanukkah with eight nights of lights, and Yom Kippur, the holiest day, for fasting and saying sorry.
explain: The central Jewish prayer is the Shema, "Hear, O Israel: the Lord our God, the Lord is one", which Jesus quoted when asked for the greatest commandment.
explain: Jewish people have suffered terrible hatred, called antisemitism, including the Holocaust; for a child today this means learning the truth, never joining in mocking, and standing up for Jewish friends.
fact: Judaism is the faith of the Jewish people, who believe in one God.
fact: Jews believe God made a covenant with Abraham and gave the Torah to Moses.
fact: Jesus was Jewish, and Christianity grew out of Judaism.
fact: The Torah is the first five books of the Hebrew Bible, handwritten on scrolls that are read in the synagogue with great respect.
fact: The synagogue is the Jewish place of prayer and study, and a rabbi is a teacher and leader.
fact: Shabbat, the Jewish Sabbath, lasts from Friday sunset to Saturday sunset, with candles, a family meal and prayer.
fact: Passover, or Pesach, is a spring festival remembering the Exodus from Egypt, with the Seder meal and matzah, a flat bread made without yeast.
fact: At the Seder the story is told to children, who ask questions about why this night is different.
fact: Hanukkah is the eight-day festival of lights, with one more candle lit each night.
fact: Yom Kippur, in autumn, is the holiest day of the Jewish year, a day of fasting and saying sorry.
fact: The Shema says: "Hear, O Israel: the Lord our God, the Lord is one."
fact: Kosher means allowed by Jewish food law; kosher food laws include no pork and not mixing meat and milk.
fact: Antisemitism is hatred of Jewish people; it led to the Holocaust, in which about six million Jews were murdered.
fact: Some antisemitism was done by people calling themselves Christians; Christians today reject it and must stand against it.
exercise: What do Jews believe about God? | There is one God | There are many gods | God is only a story | God lives only in the Temple
exercise: With whom did God make a covenant, according to Judaism? | Abraham | Noah's sons | King Herod | The Pope
exercise: Was Jesus Jewish? | Yes, Jesus was Jewish | No, Jesus was Roman | No, Jesus was Greek | No, Jesus was Egyptian
exercise: What is the Torah? | The first five books of the Hebrew Bible | The four Gospels about Jesus | A book of Christian hymns | The letters of Saint Paul
exercise: How is a Torah scroll made? | It is written by hand | It is printed by machine | It is carved in stone | It is typed on a computer
exercise: What is a synagogue? | The Jewish place of prayer and study | A Jewish shop for kosher food | A Christian church built specially for Jewish people | A school only for rabbis
exercise: What is a rabbi? | A Jewish teacher and leader | A Jewish king | A kind of Jewish prayer | A Christian priest who leads Mass
exercise: When does Shabbat begin and end? | Friday sunset to Saturday sunset | Saturday morning to Sunday morning | Sunday sunset to Monday sunset | Friday noon to Saturday noon
exercise: What does Passover remember? | The Exodus from Egypt | The birth of Abraham | The building of the Temple | The flood in Noah's time
exercise: What flat bread is eaten at Passover? | Matzah | Pitta | Naan | Bagel
exercise: Why do children ask questions at the Seder? | So the story of freedom is passed on to them | So they can win a prize for the best answer at the end | So the meal can start without the adults | So the rabbi can test them for school
exercise: How long does Hanukkah last? | Eight days | One day | Forty days | Seven weeks
exercise: What is Yom Kippur? | The holiest day, for fasting and saying sorry | A happy feast with presents | The spring festival of freedom | The weekly day of rest
exercise: What are the first words of the Shema? | Hear, O Israel | Our Father in heaven | In the beginning | The Lord is my shepherd
exercise: Which is a kosher food law? | Not mixing meat and milk | Eating no bread on Fridays | Eating fish only on Mondays | Drinking no water at Shabbat
exercise: What is antisemitism? | Hatred of Jewish people | A Jewish festival of lights | A Jewish prayer for peace | A kind of kosher food
exercise: What should Christians today do about antisemitism? | Reject it and stand against it | Ignore it as somebody else's problem | Join in if friends are doing it | Only talk about it at Easter
test: Josh says Jesus was a Christian, not a Jew. Fix it. | Jesus was Jewish; Christianity grew out of Judaism | Josh is right, Jesus was the first Christian only | Jesus was Roman, so neither is true | Jesus became Jewish only at the very end of his life on earth
test: your Jewish mate can't come to footy on Saturday morning. why? | It is Shabbat, a day of rest from Friday to Saturday sunset | It is Yom Kippur every single Saturday | Jews may never play any sport at all, on any day of the week | Saturday is the day of the Passover Seder
test: Class party, Hannah is Jewish and keeps kosher. What should you NOT give her? | A ham sandwich | A cheese sandwich | An apple | A glass of orange juice
test: A classmate says the Torah is just "the Jewish version of the New Testament". What is right? | It is the first five books of the Hebrew Bible, in the Christian Old Testament too | It is a Jewish book about the life of Jesus | It is the same as the four Gospels | It is a new book written after the Holocaust
test: Your sis thinks Hanukkah is "Jewish Christmas". What is more accurate? | It is the eight-day festival of lights, a festival of its own | It is the day Jews celebrate the birth of Jesus with presents and a tree | It is the holiest Jewish day of fasting | It is the spring festival of the Exodus
test: Some kid at school says a nasty joke about Jews and everyone laughs. What should you do? | Not laugh, say it is not OK, and tell a teacher | Laugh along so they do not pick on you next instead | Tell another joke about a different group to change the subject | Say nothing because it is only a joke and jokes never hurt anyone
test: Why did Jesus quote the Shema? | He was asked for the greatest commandment | He wanted to start a new festival | He was reading at a Christian church | He was teaching the kosher food laws to his disciples
test: A Jewish friend asks why you go to church on Sunday, not Saturday. Which is true? | Christians keep Sunday because Jesus rose on a Sunday | Christians think Saturday is an unlucky day | Christians were banned from keeping Saturday by Moses himself | Christians never had a Sabbath at all
test: why does that kid fast all day and skip lunch in autumn, weird | It may be Yom Kippur, a day of fasting and saying sorry | It is Hanukkah, when no food is allowed | It is Passover, when Jews fast for a week | It is Shabbat, when Jews skip lunch and fast every single week
test: The judge asks: "Why should Christians learn about the Holocaust?" | To remember the victims and stand against hatred today | Because it is only a story from long ago | To blame all Germans who live today | Because it happened only to Christians
test: Which question fits the Seder best? | Why is this night different from all other nights? | Why do we light one more candle each night? | Why do we fast all day without water? | Why do we stop work on Friday night?
exam: Identify the scriptures constituting the first five books of the Hebrew Bible. | The Torah | The Gospels | The Psalms | The Epistles
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the duration of Shabbat. | From sunset on Friday until sunset on Saturday | From dawn on Saturday until dawn on Sunday | From noon on Friday until noon on Saturday | From sunset on Sunday until sunset on Monday
exam: Ascertain the event commemorated by the festival of Passover. | The Exodus of the Israelites from Egypt | The covenant made with Noah after the flood | The dedication of the Temple with lights | The giving of the Shema to Abraham
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises antisemitism? | Hostility and hatred directed against Jewish people | A dietary law observed by Jewish households at every meal | A festival observed in the synagogue each autumn by the congregation | A form of Jewish prayer recited at morning and evening services
